    "severity": [
      "high",
      "critical"
    ],
    "explanation": "The migration deliberately relaxes uniqueness to live accounts only (WHERE deleted_at IS NULL), and signup.py is correctly updated to permit re-registration over a soft-deleted row. But repo.get_by_email() still queries with no deleted_at filter and calls .one_or_none() under a docstring that asserts 'emails are unique'. As soon as one user re-registers a soft-deleted address -- the exact flow this migration exists to enable -- that email has two rows and every get_by_email() call for it raises MultipleResultsFound. The new restore flow even ships a correctly-filtered helper (find_live_by_email, with a deleted_at IS NULL filter) for its own duplicate check -- the migration's author clearly knew the invariant changed -- but the old helper right above it was left unfixed. The blast radius includes request_password_reset and login (both shown here, each touched only incidentally) plus anything else routed through the helper, and it appears only for re-registered addresses, making it look like a flaky prod-only error. The defect is established only by combining the migration's partial index, the helper's unfiltered .one_or_none(), and signup's new re-registration path."
  },
